window.docContents[210].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Propagation-and-characteristics--Characteristic-geometry":{"contents":"A first-order PDE  collapses to an ODE along curves\ncalled characteristics, determined by the coefficient field . You solve\nthose ODEs and invert the characteristic map back to physical coordinates, and\nyou have a local solution wherever the construction is regular and the map\nremains invertible. Loss of invertibility is one way this local construction\ncan break down.\n\nFor a one-dimensional quasilinear system the characteristic speeds are the\neigenvalues of the coefficient or flux-Jacobian matrix. Hyperbolicity means\nthose speeds are real and the eigenbasis is complete; strict hyperbolicity\nmeans they are also distinct. Each eigenpair gives a characteristic field.\nWhether a matching Riemann invariant exists is a further question, requiring\nintegrability conditions, and the p-system section later constructs them for\nthat particular model.\n\nThe geometry takes a different form for each equation.\n\n* Transport equation (): the solution is\n  constant along characteristics, so  (Evans, 2010,\n  pp. 18–19).* Wave equation (): the two families of\n  characteristics  give d'Alembert's formula\n  (Evans, 2010, pp. 65–69).* Conservation laws (): characteristics travel at speed\n  , which depends on the solution itself. Compressive initial data can\n  drive them together and cause gradient blow-up in finite time. Classical\n  regularity then fails; an entropy continuation may contain shocks. This\n  geometric breakdown motivates the move from classical to weak to entropy\n  solutions.* Hamilton–Jacobi (): the characteristics of the\n  associated scalar conservation law determine the gradient in one space\n  dimension while the solution is smooth; recovering the solution by spatial\n  integration also requires fixing its time-dependent additive constant\n  (Evans, 2010, p. 144; Crandall and Lions,\n  1983).\n\nFor comparison, the heat equation\n has a parabolic principal symbol, not a real characteristic cone,\nso nothing limits how fast it propagates. Its Gaussian kernel is positive\neverywhere at every positive time, so nonnegative, nonzero continuous\ncompactly supported data give a positive solution everywhere immediately.\nSign-changing data can cancel at individual points. Hyperbolic equations have\nfinite domains of dependence, set by their characteristic velocities.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Propagation, shocks, and weak selection\u0009Propagation and characteristics","header":"7.1.1.1. Characteristic geometry","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Propagation-and-characteristics--Characteristic-geometry"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--From-an-Infimum-to-a-Minimizer":{"contents":"The direct method proves a minimizer exists without ever writing one down. You\nstart with a sequence  satisfying\n\n\n\nwhere  is the admissible class. The sequence need not converge strongly,\nand its limit need not be available in closed form. The proof instead chooses a\ntopology weak enough to provide compactness and strong enough for the energy to\nsurvive passage to the limit.\n\nThe old Dirichlet principle assumed that an energy infimum was attained and\nthen used the minimizer to solve a boundary-value problem. Weierstrass's\nobjections exposed the missing existence argument: continuity alone on an\ninfinite-dimensional function space does not force an infimum to be attained.\nHilbert, Tonelli, and later Morrey supplied conditions under which the\nprinciple becomes valid. Evans's Chapter 8 presents the modern Sobolev-space\nargument through weak convergence, coercivity, and lower semicontinuity.\n\nIn a reflexive space, the argument has the following form:\n\n\n\n\n\nThe panels isolate two parts of the argument. The scalar energy shows how\ncoercivity confines a minimizing sequence. The oscillating functions explain\nwhy we may need weak convergence: their norms do not decay, even though\ntheir pairing with the displayed test function tends to zero. That one\npairing illustrates the distinction; weak convergence requires the\ncorresponding limit against every continuous linear functional.\n\nCoercivity prevents escape to infinity. If\n\n\n\nthen every sufficiently low sublevel set is bounded. Reflexivity turns that\nbound into a weakly convergent subsequence. Weak closedness keeps the limit\ninside , and weak lower semicontinuity gives\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method","header":"9.3.1.1. From an Infimum to a Minimizer","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--From-an-Infimum-to-a-Minimizer"}});